{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbZL4hWG5tVY1ktMfwtr", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7296, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7296}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4912d724-ab79-40c4-b738-7728e2847427", "uuid": "03bc81a6-9842-4b0d-98af-e65371d84d8f", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T12:59:35.031Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbZL4Mg7WmeouE3o2VPy"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbZL4hWG5tVY1ktMfwtr", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The main difference is that **lists are mutable** and **tuples are immutable**. You can change a list after you create it. You can't change a tuple.\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` or `1, 2, 3` |\n| Mutable | Yes: you can append, remove and reassign items | No: fixed once created |\n| Hashable | No | Yes, if all its elements are hashable |\n| Methods | Many (`append`, `extend`, `insert`, `pop`, `sort`, \u2026) | Two (`count`, `index`) |\n| Memory / speed | A little larger, with spare capacity for growth | A little smaller and faster to create |\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 10        # fine\nnums.append(4)      # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 10       # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n### What this means in practice\n\n- **Dict keys and set members:** tuples can be used as dict keys or set members because they're hashable. Lists can't.\n  ```python\n  grid = {(0, 0): \"start\", (3, 4): \"goal\"}   # works\n  {[0, 0]: \"start\"}                           # TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n  ```\n- **The intent is different:**\n  - **Lists** usually hold a variable number of items of the same kind, like a list of users or a queue of tasks.\n  - **Tuples** usually hold a fixed group of values where each position has a meaning, like `(x, y)`, `(name, age)`, or several values returned from a function.\n- **Safety:** passing a tuple means the function you call can't modify your data in place.\n\n### Things that often trip people up\n\n- **A single-element tuple needs a trailing comma:** `(5,)` is a tuple, but `(5)` is just the integer 5.\n- **Immutability is shallow:** a tuple that contains a list can't swap that list out, but the list itself can still change.\n  ```python\n  t = ([1, 2], 3)\n  t[0].append(99)   # works: t is now ([1, 2, 99], 3)\n  ```\n  A tuple like this is also unhashable, because one of its elements is a list.\n- **Named tuples:** for record-like data, `collections.namedtuple` or `typing.NamedTuple` let you access fields by name (`p.x`) and keep the benefits of a tuple.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** use a list when the collection will grow, shrink or change. Use a tuple for a fixed group of related values, or when you need something hashable."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7296, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7296}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4912d724-ab79-40c4-b738-7728e2847427", "uuid": "9e86d669-8788-4679-8f2d-22cc64258982", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T12:59:42.773Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbZL4Mg7WmeouE3o2VPy"}
